WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:48.050
We're traveling through a beautiful but dangerous
region.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:54.329
Near the South Sudanese border in the impoverished
lowland of Ethiopia lies Gambela: a hotbed

00:00:54.329 --> 00:01:00.210
of conflicts among different ethnic groups.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.250
Behind that's South Sudan, a young, independent
nation.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.950
But the fighting there continues.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:10.680
More than two million people have fled to
neighboring countries like Ethiopia.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:16.440
This country produces hundreds of thousands
of displaced people itself – due to ethnic

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.280
clashes, drought and flooding.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.560
So what’s the perspective for refugees here?

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.360
Ethiopia has become Africa’s second largest
refugee-hosting country.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:35.140
In the Gambela region, refugees number almost
as many as Ethiopians: about 400 thousand.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:42.659
This camp is home to 60 thousand traumatized
refugees who fled fighting in South Sudan.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:51.850
“When the war erupted in South Sudan, children
were set on fire, and many others were abducted.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.500
The soldiers even killed blind people.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:01.090
People were forced to eat dead bodies and
have sexual intercourse with family members.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.200
Boys were forced to have sex with their mothers.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.049
This trauma will not be forgotten.”

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:11.430
Ruey and her family feels safe in this camp.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.910
It's supposed to be a “temporary” home
– but they've been here four years already.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:21.440
Without permission to work outside the camp,
she fears the future looks dim.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.540
“When my husband was in South Sudan he had
a job, but now he doesn’t.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.010
This is a problem because we depend on aid.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.030
Lack of education is also a problem.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.489
I want education for my children and for myself.

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:40.710
Only then will we have a chance here in Ethiopia.”

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.410
That's just one of the problems in the camp
run by the UN and the Ethiopian government.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:54.670
“When you compare the number of refugees
with the service, it is less than the standards

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.810
set out by the UN commission for refugees.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.500
More people means less resources.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:13.569
We are only providing basics, which is life
saving service.”

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:14.569
Food?

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:15.569
Yes.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:20.569
Food and water.

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:24.819
Outside the camp, there are mixed feelings
among the local population.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:29.519
Ethiopia's ethnic Nuer are more welcoming
of refugees because many arriving from South Sudan

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:31.769
are from the same group.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:36.830
But Ethiopia's ethnic Anuak say they feel
threatened.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.790
“Many of these refugees come from South
Sudan.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.959
They're straining our resources.

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:47.709
We are getting poorer, so the government should
help us, too.

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:54.409
If not, there could be clashes with the refugees.”

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:59.069
Here in Gambela city, the new Prime Minister
Abiy Ahmed has promised opportunities for

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:01.500
everyone -- refugees AND locals.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.569
But unemployment is very high in the region,
especially among young people.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:14.360
“We don't see any improvement regarding
the youth.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.620
There are no jobs.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:22.300
We're trying to get ahead on our own, but
we don’t get very far.”

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.400
Playing pool costs 5 birr, or 20 US cents.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.000
It's the only form of entertainment these youth can afford.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.860
The lack of prospects drove Ethiopians to the streets to demand jobs last year.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.300
If employment prospects aren't forthcoming soon,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:42.900
existing tensions over land and ethnicity could boil over.

